.Fn xtc_proc_at_exit "void (*fn)(void *)" "void *arg"
.Ft struct xtc_mctx *
.Fn xtc_proc_mctx "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_down_decode "const void *msg" "size_t len" "xtc_pid_t *out_pid" "int *out_reason"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_down_decode_ex "const void *msg" "size_t len" "xtc_down_info_t *out"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
xtc processes are BEAM-style lightweight actors: each has an
identity
.Pq Vt xtc_pid_t ,
a private mailbox, and a stack-backed coroutine that runs
co-operatively inside an
.Xr xtc_loop 3 .
Communication is by message passing.  Failures are isolated by
.Xr xtc_supervisor 3
or by manual link/monitor.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_proc_spawn
creates a new process running
.Fa fn ( arg ) .
The process runs on
.Fa loop
co-operatively with all other procs and tasks on that loop.
.Fa opts
is optional and may set the mailbox capacity, initial stack size,
or per-proc resource accountant.
.Pp
A process is pinned to
.Fa loop
for its lifetime: it runs only on that loop and is never migrated by
work-stealing.  This affinity is guaranteed and requires no opt-in --
only raw
.Xr xtc_exec 3
tasks are stealable.  Embedders that pin a worker to one loop (e.g. a
PostgreSQL backend per loop) can rely on it.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_proc_spawn_link
and
.Fn xtc_proc_spawn_monitor
are the atomic spawn-and-relate primitives, mirroring Erlang's
.Fn spawn_link
and
.Fn spawn_monitor .
They behave exactly like
.Fn xtc_proc_spawn ,
except that the parent<->child relationship is established BEFORE the
child is made runnable, so there is no window in which the child
exists but is not yet linked or monitored.  Even a child that runs and
exits immediately therefore delivers its
.Sq EXIT
(link) or
.Sq DOWN
(monitor) to the parent, never the
.Dv XTC_DOWN_NOPROC
"monitor raced a dead target" outcome that a
.Fn xtc_proc_spawn
followed by a separate
.Fn xtc_link
or
.Fn xtc_monitor
can hit.
.Fn xtc_proc_spawn_link
establishes a bidirectional link (as if
.Fn xtc_link
were called on the child the instant it is born);
.Fn xtc_proc_spawn_monitor
establishes a unidirectional monitor and returns its reference in
.Fa out_ref .
Both require the CALLER to be a process
.Pq Fn xtc_self No != Dv XTC_PID_NONE
and return
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_self
.Pp
.Fn xtc_send
appends an envelope holding a copy of
.Fa data\^\& [ 0 .. size )
to the mailbox of
.Fa target .
Returns
.Dv XTC_OK
on success,
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
if the target is dead, or
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
if the target's mailbox is full and bounded.
Sending is asynchronous: the send returns immediately whether or
not the receiver is currently scheduled.
.Pp
Mailboxes are bounded by
.Fa mailbox_cap
(default 4096) so that a fast sender cannot drive the receiver
out of memory.
A caller that ignores an
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
return silently loses the message.
Senders therefore MUST handle it: retry later, shed and account the
load, apply end-to-end flow control, or treat it as fatal on a
must-deliver path.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_recv
delivers the next message from the calling proc's mailbox in
arrival order.  If the mailbox is empty,
.Fa timeout_ns
controls behaviour:
.Bl -tag -width "timeout_ns < 0" -offset indent
.It Fa timeout_ns < 0
Block forever.
.It Fa timeout_ns == 0
Return
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
immediately if no message is queued.
.It Fa timeout_ns > 0
Block up to that many nanoseconds, then return
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
on timeout.
.El
The delivered buffer is owned by the caller and must be
.Xr free 3 'd
when no longer needed.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_recv_match
performs selective receive: the callback
.Fa match_fn
is invoked for each envelope in arrival order; the first one for
which it returns 1 is delivered.  Non-matching envelopes are
parked in a save queue and re-checked on the next call only if
the predicate has changed (BEAM-style recv-mark optimisation).
.Pp
.Fn xtc_recv_correlate
collects
.Fa n_expected
messages whose first
.Fa corr_size
bytes match
.Fa corr_value .
Useful for fork-join patterns where the parent assigns each
child a correlation id and waits for replies tagged with that id.
The collected messages are written to
.Fa out_msgs ;
.Fa *out_n
is set to the count actually delivered (which may be less than
.Fa n_expected
if the timeout fires first).
.Pp
.Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd
blocks until any of:
.Bl -bullet -compact -offset indent
.It
.Fa fd
becomes ready in any
.Fa interest
direction
.Pq Dv XTC_IO_READABLE | XTC_IO_WRITABLE | XTC_IO_HUP | XTC_IO_ERR ,
.It
a message arrives in the calling proc's mailbox
.Pq Dv XTC_WAIT_MAILBOX
.It
the timeout elapses
.Pq Dv XTC_WAIT_TIMEOUT
.It
the proc is killed via
.Fn xtc_exit_pid .
.El
.Fa *out_revents
receives a bitmask of which conditions fired.  This is the
canonical way to write an idle-CPU-zero server: the proc sleeps
until something real happens.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_proc_sleep
parks the calling proc on a timer for at least
.Fa ns
nanoseconds.  The loop runs other work meanwhile -- it does not block
the thread -- and, unlike a timed
.Fn xtc_recv ,
it does not touch the mailbox.  Returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
if not called from a proc.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_exit_self
terminates the calling process; cleanup runs and any links /
monitors fire.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_exit_pid
terminates another process by pid.  Asynchronous; the target
notices on its next yield or
.Fn xtc_recv
parking point.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_proc_wake
resumes a process parked in
.Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd
or
.Fn xtc_recv ,
from ANY OS thread -- including one libxtc does not manage (an
embedder's I/O-worker thread, a foreign carrier).  It is the explicit
"poke the target loop" primitive: after a foreign thread makes a watched
condition true (writes a self-pipe an fd-park watches, sets an embedder
latch, completes an async read), calling
.Fn xtc_proc_wake
nudges the target loop out of its I/O wait so the parked proc
re-evaluates its condition.  It delivers no message and asserts no wake
cause; the woken proc simply re-checks (its
.Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd
re-checks fd readiness and the mailbox, its
.Fn xtc_recv
re-checks the mailbox), so a spurious wake is always safe.  Relying on
fd readiness alone to wake a loop is correct for a condition libxtc
itself produces, but when the readiness is produced by a fully foreign
thread an embedder should pair it with
.Fn xtc_proc_wake
for a guaranteed, race-free resume.  Returns
.Dv XTC_OK
(including the no-op cases: the target is not parked, already runnable,
or gone), or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for
.Dv XTC_PID_NONE .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_link
creates a bidirectional link.  If either end exits with a
non-normal reason, the other receives an
.Sq EXIT
signal in its mailbox.
.Fn xtc_unlink
removes the link.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_monitor
creates a unidirectional monitor.  The watcher receives a
.Sq DOWN
message
.Po Vt {kind='D', ref, pid, reason} Pc
when the monitored process exits.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_proc_mailbox_stats
snapshots a process's mailbox into
.Fa out :
the current
.Va depth ,
the selective-receive
.Va saved
count, the high-water
.Va peak ,
the
.Va cap ,
and lifetime
.Va recv_total
and
.Va drop_total
counts (messages accepted and rejected).  It is safe to call from any
thread and is the basis for an overload / head-of-line-buildup scrape.
.Pp
The capacity bound applies to
.Va depth No + Va saved ,
not the mailbox alone: a selective receiver that moves non-matching
messages aside still counts them, so a flood of non-matching traffic
cannot grow a process without limit by draining the mailbox into the
save queue.  A consequence is that a process selectively waiting for a
message while its budget fills with non-matching ones will see further
sends rejected with
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
(including the awaited one): the bound is a hard backpressure contract,
so design selective waits with that in mind (prefer
.Fn xtc_recv_correlate
and bounded timeouts).
.Pp
A process can also be given a mailbox watermark via
.Va xtc_proc_opts_t.mailbox_watermark_pct
(1..100; 0 disables) plus
.Va mailbox_watermark_fn
and
.Va mailbox_watermark_user .
When an accepted message first brings the depth to that percent of the
cap, the callback fires once on the rising edge -- so an application
can shed load before the hard cap rejects with
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN .
The callback runs on the sender's thread outside the mailbox lock and
must not block.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_fault_guard_install
installs a process-wide handler for the synchronous, fiber-directed
faults
.Dv SIGSEGV , SIGBUS , SIGFPE
and
.Dv SIGILL
on an alternate signal stack.
.Pp
Fault containment is PER-OS-THREAD:
.Fn xtc_fault_guard_install
must have run on every OS thread that hosts a loop before a fiber
fault on that thread can be contained and turned into a DOWN.  A
common idiom is to install it at the entry of one long-lived fiber per
loop thread (a supervisor); the call is idempotent, so repeated
installs are harmless.  A loop thread on which the guard was never
installed lets a fiber fault take down the whole process rather than
containing it.
.Fn xtc_proc_recovery_arm
arms a recovery frame for the calling process (used like
.Xr sigsetjmp 3 :
it returns 0 normally and the fault signal number when a contained
fault returns control there).  On such a fault the handler identifies
the faulting process -- it is the faulting thread's running process --
and, if it armed a recovery frame and is not in a critical section,
unwinds only that fiber back to the frame; siblings on the loop are
untouched.  The recovered process releases its resources and calls
.Fn xtc_exit_self ,
so a supervisor observes the fault as a normal
.Sq DOWN .
.Fn xtc_proc_critical_enter
and
.Fn xtc_proc_critical_leave
bracket a region in which a fault must NOT be contained -- shared
state may be torn -- so it escalates to process abort, preserving the
embedder's critical-section panic semantics.  The recovery frame only
unwinds the call stack; locks, fds, allocations and other resources
held at fault time are the recovery block's responsibility to
release.  Containment is POSIX-only; on Windows the calls compile but
a fault keeps its process-wide disposition.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_proc_at_exit
registers a callback to run when the calling process exits -- on a
normal return or a contained-fault recovery -- LIFO, outside signal
context, and before the proc's monitors observe its
.Sq DOWN .
It is where an embedder guarantees a faulted session releases what it
held: register the lock manager's release-all so no lock outlives the
proc, close fds, or reset a memory context.
.Fn xtc_proc_mctx
returns a memory context scoped to the process, created on first use
and destroyed automatically at exit -- a backstop so a session's
allocations are reclaimed even if its recovery block faults.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_down_decode
extracts the target pid and exit reason from a
.Sq DOWN
signal.  The DOWN and EXIT signals are sent PACKED; a consumer that
casts the message to its own unpacked mirror struct misreads the
fields (the
.Va reason
in particular).  Use this helper rather than hand-rolling the layout.
.Sh MIGRATION
By default a proc's coroutine is
.Em pinned
to the loop it was spawned on: it is never work-stolen, and
.Fn xtc_self
and the recovery frame always run on that one carrier thread.
Setting
.Fa migratable
to 1 in
.Vt xtc_proc_opts_t
makes the coroutine work-stealable: on a multi-loop
.Xr xtc_exec 3
executor, an idle loop may steal a
.Em parked or scheduled
proc to rebalance runnable work across cores.
A proc is stolen only at a yield/park point, never mid-instruction, so
no statement ever runs half on one loop and half on another.
.Pp
A zeroed
.Vt xtc_proc_opts_t
leaves
.Fa migratable
at 0 (pinned) -- byte-identical to the historical behavior.
.Pp
Migration preserves everything that defines the proc: its
.Fa pid ,
.Fn xtc_self ,
its links / monitors and the
.Dv DOWN
signal, its recovery frame, and its mailbox.
The carrier loop can change, and only at a yield point.
This is proven under the deterministic simulator
.Pq test_sim_migratable :
migratable procs are stolen across loops while
.Fn xtc_self
identity and
.Dv DOWN
delivery are asserted to survive every migration, with byte-identical
replay.
.Pp
The one caller obligation: a migratable proc must not hold
thread-affine state across a yield and then assume it is still on the
same OS thread after resuming.
The runtime carries the proc's own identity across the move; it cannot
know about
.Em your
thread-local or CPU-affine state.
If the proc reads no such state across a yield (the common case), it is
unconditionally safe to make migratable.
.Pp
When a consumer DOES keep per-proc state that must track the running
proc across a migration, hang it off the proc itself with
.Fn xtc_proc_set_userdata
(read back with
.Fn xtc_proc_userdata ) :
the pointer belongs to the proc, not the loop or OS thread, so it
rides along across a carrier change exactly like the mailbox and
.Fn xtc_self .
Both operate on the calling proc (keyed implicitly by
.Fn xtc_self ) ,
are O(1), allocation-free, and lock-free.
The pointer is NULL by default; the runtime never dereferences or frees
it (the consumer owns whatever it points to, the same lifetime contract
as
.Fn xtc_proc_at_exit Ap s
.Fa arg ) ;
it is dropped when the proc exits.
.Fn xtc_proc_set_userdata
returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
when called off a proc, and
.Fn xtc_proc_userdata
returns
.Dv NULL
there.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_proc_set_class
places the calling proc's task in a proportional-share scheduling
class (created with
.Xr xtc_exec_class_create 3
on the proc's loop), the runtime-side equivalent of setting
.Va xtc_proc_opts_t.sched_class
at spawn.  The class weights the proc's CPU share against the other
classes on its loop; a
.Dv NULL
handle resets it to the default (implicit) class.  Returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
called off a proc or with a handle that is not on the calling proc's
loop.  See
.Xr xtc_exec 3
for the proportional-share model (INSPIRED BY Glommio).
.Pp
A consumer that caches CARRIER-AFFINE state across a yield -- e.g. an
fd/eventfd added to a wait set that is itself per-loop, so the
registration must be re-armed if the fiber resumes elsewhere -- detects
a migration with
.Xr xtc_exec 3 Ns 's
.Fn xtc_exec_loop_id :
it reads the CURRENT resume's carrier
.Pq re-established every resume, like Fn xtc_self ,
so capturing it before a yield and comparing after tells the consumer
whether it moved:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
int before = xtc_exec_loop_id();
xtc_yield();                      /* may resume on a different loop */
if (xtc_exec_loop_id() != before)
        /* re-arm any per-loop wait registration here */
.Ed
.Pp
This needs no dedicated "you migrated" callback: the carrier id is
already a plain, O(1), thread-local read, so the check costs one
comparison at exactly the safe points a consumer already controls (its
own yield/park call sites) -- there is no separate hook to remember to
wire up, and no case where a resume can happen without the consumer's
own code running first.  A proc that never registers carrier-affine
state (the common case, and true of the mailbox, userdata, and
recovery frame this section covers) needs no such check at all.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
All entry points return
.Dv XTC_OK
on success.  Negative values are stable
.Dv XTC_E_*
codes; see
.Xr xtc_strerror 3 .
.Sh EXAMPLES
A request-reply pattern with selective receive and timeouts:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static void server(void *arg) {
    void *msg; size_t sz;
    for (;;) {
        if (xtc_recv(&msg, &sz, 5000LL * 1000 * 1000) != XTC_OK)
            continue;
        /* ... handle msg, reply via xtc_send to msg->reply_to ... */
        free(msg);
    }
}

xtc_pid_t pid;
xtc_proc_spawn(loop, server, NULL, NULL, &pid);
.Ed
